          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A person with blood group O has a non-functional enzyme encoded by the ABO locus</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A person with blood group O has a non-functional enzyme encoded by the ABO locus. Which antigen is present on their red blood cells?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>A antigen</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The A antigen requires a functional A-allele enzyme that adds N-acetylgalactosamine to H substance. Group O lacks this enzyme, so no A antigen is formed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>H antigen</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The O allele encodes a non-functional enzyme, so H substance — built by fucosyltransferase adding fucose to the precursor sugar chain — remains unmodified on the red cell surface.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>B antigen</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The B antigen requires a functional B-allele enzyme that adds galactose to H substance. Group O lacks this enzyme, so no B antigen is formed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>No antigen is present on group O red cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Group O red cells are not antigen-free. They retain the H antigen, the precursor structure from which A and B antigens would normally be built. This is why Bombay phenotype individuals, who lack even H antigen, cannot receive group O blood.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the biosynthetic pathway: the H gene adds fucose to form H substance, then the ABO gene modifies it. The O allele is non-functional, so H substance remains unchanged — group O red cells carry H antigen."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Group O individuals retain unmodified H antigen because the O allele cannot add further sugar residues to H substance."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
