          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following is the commonest infectious cause of transfusion-related </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following is the commonest infectious cause of transfusion-related mortality?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatitis B virus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>HBV is an important transfusion-transmitted infection, but mandatory HBsAg screening has significantly reduced its incidence. The leading infectious killer is bacterial, not viral.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>HIV</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fourth-generation HIV ELISA combined with nucleic acid testing has made HIV transmission exceedingly rare. The commonest infectious cause of transfusion death is bacterial contamination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatitis C virus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Anti-HCV antibody screening and NAT have dramatically reduced HCV transmission. The leading infectious cause of transfusion mortality is not viral at all.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bacterial contamination of platelet units</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Platelets are stored at room temperature (20–24 °C), which allows skin flora introduced from the venepuncture site to multiply to dangerous levels. This makes bacterial contamination the commonest infectious cause of transfusion-related death.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key insight: platelets are stored at room temperature, unlike other components. Skin flora from the needle site can proliferate, making bacterial contamination — not any virus — the leading infectious cause of transfusion death."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Room-temperature storage creates an ideal environment for bacterial growth — the single biggest infectious threat in modern blood banking despite all viral screening."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
