          "label": "<p>A patient develops acute dyspnoea and bilateral pulmonary infiltrates during a r</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient develops acute dyspnoea and bilateral pulmonary infiltrates during a red-cell transfusion. Which finding best distinguishes transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) from transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO)?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Presence of fever and rigors</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fever can occur in both TRALI and TACO and is not a reliable discriminator. The key bedside differentiator is jugular venous pressure, which reflects the underlying mechanism.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bilateral infiltrates on chest radiograph</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both TRALI and TACO produce bilateral pulmonary infiltrates — the chest radiograph alone cannot distinguish them. The haemodynamic profile is the key.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Normal jugular venous pressure</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. TRALI is non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema — donor antibodies activate recipient pulmonary neutrophils, flooding the alveoli, but JVP remains normal. TACO is volume-driven overload, so JVP is raised. This single bedside sign separates the two conditions.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Onset within the first 10 minutes of transfusion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both TRALI and TACO are acute reactions occurring within hours of transfusion. Timing of onset does not reliably distinguish them; JVP assessment does.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The critical distinction: TRALI is non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema (immune-mediated, JVP normal), while TACO is cardiogenic (volume overload, JVP raised). JVP is the single most reliable bedside differentiator."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well analysed. TRALI is immune-mediated (donor antibodies → neutrophil activation → capillary leak, normal JVP), while TACO is volume overload (raised JVP). Both cause dyspnoea and bilateral infiltrates, but JVP separates them."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
